Chronoliturgic Decay
Ingredients: BIT MUSIC (CHIPTUNE) + FOLK ROCK + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Risers
Creative Catalyst: It began as protest music performed on hacked traffic infrastructure.

A fractured, spectral soundscape where the mournful keening of circuit-bent game consoles weeps over the stark, skeletal strums of corroded acoustic guitars. Imagine a Gregorian chant fed through a glitching arcade machine, underpinned by the unsettling thrum of detuned digital harpsichords and the phantom clang of a forgotten bell tower. Expect sudden, disorienting shifts from fragile lo-fi melodies to cataclysmic bursts of static-laced digital thunder, all bound by an insistent, ritualistic rhythm that feels both ancient and illegally synthesized.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: Ranges from funereal dirges (60-80 BPM) to agitated, frantic stutters (130-150 BPM), often featuring polyrhythmic clashes between rigid pixelated beats and organic, stumbling folk percussion.
- Harmonic Palette: Predominantly minor keys, employing Phrygian and Locrian modes. Features heavy dissonance from detuned digital waveforms clashing with raw, untempered acoustic voicings, creating a "sacred glitch" effect.
- Production Signatures: 1) Digital Rusting: Deliberate, controlled corruption of audio files to introduce artifacting, bit-crushing, and data decay as expressive elements. 2) Spectral Looping: Capturing ambient feedback loops from ancient, rewired traffic signal processors, then stretching and pitching them into cavernous, resonant drones. 3) Ghost Amplification: Using electromagnetic fields from defunct CRT screens to "amplify" acoustic instruments, infusing their natural timbre with an otherworldly hum and buzz.
- Dynamics: Extreme and volatile. Tracks often begin with a deceptively sparse, almost fragile intimacy before escalating through slow-burn digital tension risers, culminating in cataclysmic explosions of multi-layered sonic collapse and distorted, pixelated orchestral swells, only to abruptly fall back into hushed, crackling silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the decrepit underbellings of sprawling megalopolises, where forgotten data cables snaked through abandoned transit tunnels, Chronoliturgic Decay emerged from the hands of the "Grid-Haunters." These artists, disaffected by systemic over-regulation, found their voice by physically tapping into and re-routing the antique, networked infrastructure of dead traffic lights, pedestrian signals, and abandoned highway sensors. They fashioned crude instruments from these salvaged components, conducting illicit "Signal Masses" at crossroads choked with overgrown weeds, dressed in patched-up garments adorned with reflective street sign fragments, their faces obscured by the flicker of projected signal patterns. Their rituals involved communal lamentations sung over the rhythmic, hacked chirps of crossing signals, culminating in collective acts of digital disruption that would temporarily plunge city blocks into silent, rebellious darkness, forcing a moment of unintended meditation amidst the urban chaos.

Typical Instruments:
Circuit-bent Commodore 64s and Game Boys, heavily corroded acoustic guitars strung with industrial wire, handmade banjos constructed from discarded road signs, vocal effects processed through hacked intercom systems, custom "Riser Generators" built from salvaged traffic light controllers, detuned digital harpsichords from ancient arcade PCBs, and percussion forged from traffic cones and metal road grates.

For Fans Of: Current 93, Godspeed You! Black Emperor, Oneohtrix Point Never, The Microphones
